Publish _NET_CLIENT_LIST(_STACKING) even when there is no icon manager.

EwmhAddClientWindow() decides whether a window belongs in _NET_CLIENT_LIST by
asking whether it has an entry in the ICON MANAGER:

	if(new_win->iconmanagerlist != NULL && ...)

Its own comment says why -- "Look at new_win->iconmanagerlist as an
optimization for !LookInList(Scr->IconMgrNoShow, ...)" -- and the shortcut is
sound as long as an icon manager exists.  With NoIconManagers it does not:
CreateIconManagers() and AllocateIconManager() both return early, no window
ever gets an iconmanagerlist entry, and the property stays empty for the whole
session.

That ties a standard property to an unrelated piece of ctwm's own UI.  Any
EWMH client that asks the window manager what windows exist -- a pager, a task
switcher, an external panel -- is told "none", and nothing in the EWMH
specification suggests the answer should depend on whether the window manager
happens to be drawing a window list of its own.

LikeOS hits this because its taskbar is a separate program (taskbar(1), which
reads _NET_CLIENT_LIST and drives windows with the standard client messages)
and ctwm's icon manager is switched off in system.ctwmrc.  The symptom is a
taskbar that never shows a single window.

Two changes, and the second is what makes the first safe.

1. EwmhInClientList() answers the membership question, falling back to the
   condition the shortcut stands in for when there is no icon manager to ask.
   Where an icon manager does exist nothing changes.

2. _NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING is DERIVED from _NET_CLIENT_LIST instead of
   deciding membership over again.  The two builders cannot be kept in step by
   giving them matching conditions, because they do not run at the same
   moment: AddWindow() inserts into the OTP list -- which restacks, which
   rebuilds the stacking property -- BEFORE HandleMapRequest() reaches the
   EwmhAddClientWindow() call on the very next line.  For that window the two
   answers are momentarily bound to differ, and ctwm says so on every map,
   unmap, raise and lower:

	Incorrect number of stacked windows: 4 (expected 3)

   Upstream never sees it only because iconmanagerlist happens to be filled in
   at a point that hides the window during that restack.  Deriving one list
   from the other removes the race rather than re-timing it, and it matches
   what EWMH says the two properties are: the same set of windows, ordered
   differently.

The delete side needs no change -- EwmhDeleteClientWindow() searches the list
for the window rather than re-deriving whether it should have been in it, so
it is already correct for windows added by either route.

--- a/ewmh.c
+++ b/ewmh.c
